<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Global Intentional Homicide Trends</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A Multi-Dimensional Analysis (1990–2023)</p>
    <p class="subtitle" style="opacity:0.75; font-size:0.95rem;">Evidence from UNODC Crime Trends Survey Data</p>
    <p class="meta">Report generated: April 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Audience: Policy Analysts &amp; Public Safety Stakeholders</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPI Dashboard -->
<div class="kpi-section">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(kpis['global_median_recent'], 1)}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Global Median Rate</div>
    <div class="kpi-context">per 100,000 ({most_recent_year})</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value secondary">{fmt(kpis['male_female_ratio_recent'], 1)}:1</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Ratio</div>
    <div class="kpi-context">Median across reporting countries</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value secondary">{fmt(kpis['highest_national_rate_recent']['rate'], 1)}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate</div>
    <div class="kpi-context">{kpis['highest_national_rate_recent']['country']} ({most_recent_year})</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(kpis['decade_changes'][1]['change_pct'], 1)}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">2000s → 2010s Change</div>
    <div class="kpi-context">Global mean rate shift</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value accent">{fmt(kpis['coverage_2023'], 0)}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Reporting Coverage</div>
    <div class="kpi-context">{kpis['countries_reporting_2023']} of {kpis['total_countries']} countries ({most_recent_year})</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Table of Contents -->
<div class="toc">
  <h2>Contents</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#executive-summary">Executive Summary</a></li>
    <li><a href="#global-overview">1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#regional-comparison">2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</a></li>
    <li><a href="#sex-disparity">3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</a></li>
    <li><a href="#age-group-analysis">4. Age Group Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#notable-trends">5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</a></li>
    <li><a href="#data-limitations">6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</a></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- Executive Summary -->
<section id="executive-summary">
  <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
  <p><strong>Is the world becoming safer from intentional homicide?</strong> The evidence is cautiously optimistic—but highly uneven. Between 1990 and 2023, the global <em>median</em> homicide rate declined from approximately 3.4 to <strong>1.8 per 100,000 population</strong> in the most recent reporting year. The 2000s to 2010s saw the most pronounced improvement, with the global mean falling by <strong>9.6%</strong>. However, progress has stalled since the 2010s, and the 2020s show essentially flat trends.</p>

  <p>Yet averages conceal stark disparities. Males are homicide victims at roughly <strong>three times the rate of females</strong> globally—a ratio that has remained remarkably persistent across decades. Young adult males (ages 20–29) face the highest risk of any demographic group. At the country level, a small number of Caribbean and Latin American nations continue to experience extreme homicide burdens, while several East Asian and European jurisdictions report rates below 1 per 100,000.</p>

  <div class="highlight-box warning">
    <strong>Critical caveat:</strong> These findings must be interpreted in light of significant data gaps. In 2023, only <strong>84 of 201 countries and territories</strong> (42%) reported homicide data. The unbalanced panel means year-to-year comparisons reflect both real changes and shifts in which countries are reporting.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 1: Global Overview -->
<section id="global-overview">
  <h2>1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>

  <p>The trajectory of global homicide rates over the past three decades reveals a story of broad improvement followed by plateau. The chart below tracks both the median and mean homicide rates across all reporting countries from 1990 to 2023.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['global_trend']}" alt="Global homicide rate trend 1990-2023"></div>

  <p>The median rate is the more robust indicator here, as it is less sensitive to extreme outliers (such as countries experiencing civil conflict or organized-crime surges). The median fell steadily from the early 1990s through the mid-2010s, bottoming near <strong>2.3 per 100,000</strong> around 2019, before ticking down further to <strong>1.8</strong> in 2023—though the 2023 figure is based on a reduced reporting pool.</p>

  <h3>Decade-over-Decade Trajectory</h3>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['decade_comparison']}" alt="Decade comparison of global mean homicide rates"></div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Decade</th><th class="num">Mean Rate</th><th class="num">Median Rate</th><th class="num">Change from Prior Decade</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1990s</td><td class="num">8.27</td><td class="num">3.96</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000s</td><td class="num">8.51</td><td class="num">3.33</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-up">+2.9%</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010s</td><td class="num">7.70</td><td class="num">2.62</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-down">−9.6%</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020s (to date)</td><td class="num">7.71</td><td class="num">2.35</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-stable">+0.2%</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The 2000s saw a slight <em>increase</em> in the global mean, driven in part by the inclusion of more high-violence countries in the reporting pool as data coverage expanded. The 2010s delivered the clearest improvement. Since 2020, the mean has held roughly steady, though the reduced number of reporting countries—especially from regions with higher baseline rates—may artificially depress recent figures.</p>

  <h3>Reporting Coverage</h3>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['coverage']}" alt="Data coverage over time"></div>
  <p>Coverage peaked in the mid-2010s at roughly 130 countries (≈65% of the panel), but has fallen sharply since 2020, reaching just <strong>42% in 2023</strong>. This decline is not random: lower-income and conflict-affected states are disproportionately represented among non-reporters, meaning recent global aggregates may understate true worldwide homicide levels.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Regional & Country Comparison -->
<section id="regional-comparison">
  <h2>2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>

  <p>Homicide is not distributed evenly across the globe. In the most recent reporting year ({most_recent_year}), the ten highest-rate countries were concentrated in the Caribbean and Latin America, while the lowest-rate jurisdictions were predominantly in East Asia and Southern Europe.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['top_bottom']}" alt="Top 10 highest and lowest homicide rates by country"></div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Highest Rates ({most_recent_year})</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th class="num">Rate</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
'''

for row in report_data['top10_recent']:
    html += f'          <tr><td>{row["country"]}</td><td class="num">{fmt(row["homicide_rate"], 1)}</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Lowest Rates ({most_recent_year})</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th class="num">Rate</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
'''

for row in report_data['bottom10_recent']:
    html += f'          <tr><td>{row["country"]}</td><td class="num">{fmt(row["homicide_rate"], 2)}</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Geographic pattern:</strong> Caribbean small-island states dominate the highest-rate list, with <strong>Saint Kitts and Nevis</strong> recording 64.2 per 100,000 in 2023. Latin American mainland countries (Ecuador, Honduras, Colombia, Mexico) also feature prominently. At the other extreme, city-states and wealthy East Asian nations report near-zero rates.
  </div>

  <p>The spread between the highest and lowest national rates exceeds <strong>64 per 100,000</strong>—more than two orders of magnitude. This divergence underscores that homicide is fundamentally a local and national problem, driven by country-specific factors such as rule-of-law strength, organized crime presence, youth unemployment, and firearm availability, rather than by global trends alone.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Gender Disparity -->
<section id="sex-disparity">
  <h2>3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>

  <p>One of the most consistent findings across the entire dataset is the profound gender imbalance in homicide victimization. Males are killed at substantially higher rates than females in virtually every country and year for which sex-disaggregated data exist.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['gender_ratio']}" alt="Male-to-female homicide rate ratio over time"></div>

  <p>The median male-to-female ratio across reporting countries has hovered between <strong>3:1 and 3.5:1</strong> for most of the past two decades. In 2023, the median ratio was <strong>3.2:1</strong>. The mean ratio is higher (around 4.5:1) because a subset of countries exhibit extreme ratios—driven either by very high male victimization or by extremely low female rates.</p>

  <div class="highlight-box info">
    <strong>Key insight:</strong> The male-to-female ratio has proven remarkably <em>stable</em> over time. While absolute rates have risen and fallen in many countries, the relative risk faced by men versus women has changed little. This suggests that the underlying mechanisms driving gender disparity—such as male involvement in violent offending networks, gang activity, and street violence—are structurally persistent.
  </div>

  <h3>Absolute Rates by Sex (Most Recent Year)</h3>
  <p>In 2023, the highest male homicide rates were recorded in:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Saint Kitts and Nevis</strong> — 125.0 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</strong> — 92.9 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Jamaica</strong> — 91.5 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Ecuador</strong> — 84.5 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Haiti</strong> — 74.2 per 100,000</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The highest female rates were considerably lower:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guyana</strong> — 11.6 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Saint Lucia</strong> — 9.9 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Dominica</strong> — 9.0 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Haiti</strong> — 8.7 per 100,000</li>
    <li><strong>Jamaica</strong> — 8.2 per 100,000</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="insight">Notably, while male rates in the worst-affected countries can exceed 100 per 100,000, female rates rarely surpass 12—underscoring that even in high-violence contexts, women face a categorically different (and lower) level of risk.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Age Group Analysis -->
<section id="age-group-analysis">
  <h2>4. Age Group Analysis</h2>

  <p>Age is a critical but underreported dimension of homicide risk. Because many countries do not systematically disaggregate by age, the analysis below draws on the subset of observations (primarily from the 2010s onward) where age-specific data are available.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['age_groups']}" alt="Homicide rates by age group and sex"></div>

  <p>The pattern is unmistakable: <strong>young adults face the highest homicide risk</strong>, and this is overwhelmingly driven by male victimization. For males, median rates rise steeply from childhood, peak in the <strong>25–29 and 30–44 age brackets</strong> (median ≈ 4.0 per 100,000 across all reporting years), and then decline gradually into older age. For females, the age gradient is far flatter, with median rates staying below 1.5 per 100,000 across all age groups.</p>

  <h3>Risk by Life Stage</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Age Group</th><th class="num">Female Median</th><th class="num">Male Median</th><th class="num">Male / Female Ratio</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

age = pd.DataFrame(report_data['age_summary'])
age_pivot = age.pivot(index='age_group', columns='sex', values='median_rate')
age_order = ['0_9','10_14','15_17','18_19','20_24','25_29','30_44','45_59','60_PLUS']
for ag in age_order:
    if ag in age_pivot.index:
        f_val = age_pivot.loc[ag, 'female']
        m_val = age_pivot.loc[ag, 'male']
        ratio = m_val / f_val if f_val > 0 else float('inf')
        html += f'      <tr><td>{ag.replace("_","-")}</td><td class="num">{fmt(f_val, 2)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(m_val, 2)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(ratio, 1)}:1</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="highlight-box success">
    <strong>Policy implication:</strong> Prevention programs targeting males aged 15–34 would likely yield the highest marginal reduction in homicide victimization. This age-sex intersection is where the burden is most concentrated and where early intervention, gang diversion, and employment programs may be most cost-effective.
  </div>

  <p>Children under 14 experience very low homicide rates in most reporting countries, though even here males show slightly elevated risk relative to females. The elderly (60+) see a modest uptick in risk relative to mid-life, particularly for males, which may reflect vulnerability to robbery-related violence in some contexts.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Notable Trends -->
<section id="notable-trends">
  <h2>5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</h2>

  <p>Beyond snapshots of any single year, the most compelling policy stories lie in how countries have changed over time. The following charts highlight the countries with the largest absolute increases and decreases between their first and most recent observations (minimum 10-year span and 3 data points).</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['notable_trends']}" alt="Countries with largest increases and decreases in homicide rates"></div>

  <h3>Remarkable Improvements</h3>
  <p><strong>El Salvador</strong> stands as the most dramatic turnaround. From a peak of <strong>138.4 per 100,000</strong> in 1995, the country’s rate fell to <strong>7.9</strong> by 2022—a decline of 94%. <strong>Colombia</strong> similarly reduced its rate from 85.9 (1991) to 24.9 (2023), a 67% improvement. Other notable declines include the <strong>Philippines</strong> (down 71%), <strong>Russia</strong> (down 52%), and <strong>Latvia</strong> (down 75%).</p>

  <p>These improvements are not merely statistical artifacts; they correspond to documented policy and social changes: strengthened criminal justice institutions, demobilization of armed groups, economic stabilization, and targeted violence-reduction programs in urban hotspots.</p>

  <h3>Alarming Deteriorations</h3>
  <p>On the other side, several Caribbean and Latin American nations have experienced severe deteriorations. <strong>Ecuador</strong> rose from 8.5 (1990) to 45.7 (2023). <strong>Haiti</strong> climbed from 5.2 (2007) to 41.2 (2023). <strong>Honduras</strong> increased from 9.9 (1990) to 31.4 (2023), though it has moderated from an even higher peak in the early 2010s.</p>

  <div class="highlight-box warning">
    <strong>Outlier alert:</strong> Small-island Caribbean states exhibit extreme volatility due to small populations. A handful of homicides can produce rate spikes that appear dramatic in per-capita terms. Analysts should treat rates in jurisdictions with populations under 100,000 with particular caution.
  </div>

  <h3>Contextual Factors</h3>
  <p>While this report does not attempt causal attribution, the data patterns are consistent with established criminological research:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Improvements</strong> often follow periods of institutional consolidation, economic growth, and targeted policing reforms.</li>
    <li><strong>Deteriorations</strong> frequently coincide with narcotics trafficking expansion, gang fragmentation, political instability, and erosion of judicial capacity.</li>
    <li><strong>Volatility</strong> in small states suggests that localized shocks—natural disasters, tourism collapses, or single criminal-group conflicts—can have outsized statistical effects.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Data Limitations & Recommendations -->
<section id="data-limitations">
  <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
  <p>This analysis is bounded by the structure and coverage of the UNODC dataset. Users should bear the following limitations in mind:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> Not all countries report in every year. The composition of the reporting pool changes over time, which can create spurious trends in global aggregates.</li>
    <li><strong>Coverage decline:</strong> After peaking at roughly 65% of countries in the mid-2010s, coverage has fallen to just 42% in 2023. Recent global figures are therefore less representative than those from earlier periods.</li>
    <li><strong>UK coverage:</strong> Data for the United Kingdom refer to <strong>England and Wales only</strong>. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately where available and should not be conflated with the UK figure.</li>
    <li><strong>Territories:</strong> Some dependent territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are reported separately. These should not be double-counted with their parent states.</li>
