    <li><strong>Coverage decline:</strong> After peaking at roughly 65% of countries in the mid-2010s, coverage has fallen to just 42% in 2023. Recent global figures are therefore less representative than those from earlier periods.</li>
    <li><strong>UK coverage:</strong> Data for the United Kingdom refer to <strong>England and Wales only</strong>. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately where available and should not be conflated with the UK figure.</li>
    <li><strong>Territories:</strong> Some dependent territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are reported separately. These should not be double-counted with their parent states.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing years were not interpolated. Any trend analysis that treats absent data as zero would be seriously misleading.</li>
    <li><strong>Small-population volatility:</strong> Countries and territories with very small populations can show extreme rate fluctuations from year to year that do not reflect meaningful changes in absolute violence levels.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="highlight-box info">
